  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Blade Tidwell threw five and two-thirds shutout innings. The offense did not earn a single run while he was out there. And that is what sank them here.",
    "what_this_means": "They controlled this game for eight innings. Knizner and Adames each drove in one run, and for most of the night that felt like enough. But baseball does not care about what feels sufficient at the seventh-inning stretch. The ninth inning arrived with the lead still held, runners still stranded, and the bullpen suddenly unable to find anyone at home. Sam Hentges walked into a situation he could not escape. Boston scored three times before the inning was over, and the Giants watched a two-run advantage dissolve in under a minute. Ten strikeouts silenced the lineup when it mattered most. They had their window. They swung and missed through it, and when they looked back, the game was gone.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is the cost of leaving runners stranded at critical moments. The offense gathered eight hits total, moved runners into scoring position ten separate times, and converted exactly zero of those situations into anything beyond two runs. Two runs with ten runners left on base is not execution. It is a baseline error that no pitching line, no matter how clean, can survive the length of nine innings. Tidwell deserved better. He did not get it. The bullpen failed at the last moment because the offense had already failed at every moment that came before it. This game illustrates a recurring fracture: when the hitting falls silent in the sixth and beyond, the margins disappear and the team loses games it should have held.",
